%N A398413 Number of ways the n-th prime is a Pillai prime.
%C A398413 This sequence is an extension of the idea in A063980: Pillai primes. a(n) is the number of m's which satisfies two congruences: m! + 1 == 0 (mod p) and p != 1 (mod m).
%H A398413 Robert G. Wilson v, <a href="/A398413/b398413.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A398413 a(1) is 0 since the prime 2 has no m satisfying the condition;
%e A398413 a(10) is 1 since the second Pillai prime, 29, has m equal to 18;
%e A398413 a(9) is 2 since the first Pillai prime, 23, has m equal to {14, 18};
%e A398413 a(17) is 3 since the third Pillai prime, 59, has m is equal to {15, 40, 43}; etc.
%e A398413 The array begins: The primes, p_n, are stated and not their indices, n.
